From 1c259adfa3259e2a034fffaa2a24cae6ce6d9eef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 12:58:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] reaper v1.9.9b: all-bands Professional page (single Apply for
 2.4/5/6 GHz)

New reaper-native Reaper_WiFiPro.asp replaces the Professional tab: every radio
on one page, one Apply. The stock per-band page forced one restart_wireless per
band (change 3 bands = 3 restarts); this posts every CHANGED wlN_* across all
three units and fires a single restart_wireless (all radios cycle together, the
proven Lock/Pin apply pattern). Only changed fields are written - untouched
settings, including all auth/RADIUS, are left exactly as-is.

- ~30 per-band settings as a band matrix (rows = settings, cols = 2.4/5/6 GHz):
  radio/11be/mode/agile-MB/TWT/hide-SSID/AP-isolate, max-clients/roaming,
  turbo-QAM/AMPDU/RTS/frag/disable-11b/bg-protect/preamble, OFDMA/MU-MIMO/
  beamforming x2/airtime-fairness, BT-coex/IGMP, WMM trio, DTIM/beacon/frameburst,
  TX power. Band-inapplicable cells shown disabled.
- Controls carry data-name + data-nv(<% nvram_get %>); JS marks only changed
  fields and posts them; numbers range-validated client-side.
- Swap = menuTree Professional tab -> Reaper_WiFiPro.asp. Stock
  Advanced_WAdvanced_Content.asp kept BYTE-PRISTINE as a one-line fallback
  (revert the menu line, no reflash) and still hosts Region + the Wireless
  Scheduler (deferred from v1 - regulatory/reboot + week-grid, wire later).
- i18n: setting labels reuse existing stock <#WLANConfig11b_*#> tokens (already
  localized). 14 new RWFP_* chrome strings (section titles etc.) are EN-seeded in
  all 25 dicts (lockstep 5988); 24-lang pass deferred until the design is
  metal-confirmed.

METAL-ONLY validatable (mock cannot render wl data). Verify on flash: each
setting reads its current value, a cross-band change applies in one restart,
TX power + wireless-mode changes take effect. Fallback: revert menuTree line.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
